#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# is_positive

#% text
# `is_positive(x)` gives `true` when `x` is a positive real, `false` when it is known not to be, and stays unevaluated when neither is decided.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Numbers and closed numeric expressions such as `pi - 3` are decided by numerical bounds; symbols by the assumptions in force.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
is_positive(3)
#% output
#= true

#% cell
is_positive(-2.5)
#% output
#= false

#% cell
is_positive(pi - 3)
#% output
#= true

#% cell
is_positive(x)
#% output
#= is_positive(x)

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Decided by assumptions:

#% cell
assuming(x > 1, is_positive(x - 1))
#% output
#= true

#% cell
assuming(x > 0, is_positive(x^2 + x))
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Zero is neither positive nor negative:

#% cell
[is_positive(0), is_negative(0), is_nonnegative(0)]
#% output
#= [false, false, true]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A non-real number is left undecided rather than `false`:

#% cell
is_positive(1 + im)
#% output
#= false

#% text
# A number that is not real is neither positive nor negative:

#% cell
is_positive(im)
#% output
#= false

#% cell
is_negative(im)
#% output
#= false

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `is_negative`, `is_nonnegative`, `sign`, `assuming`
